---
/* Wrapper page for examples/raw/newsletter.html.
 *
 * The artifact is NOT rendered by Astro and never will be. It is a standalone
 * document -- that is what makes it something a client can be handed -- and a
 * documentation bar welded to the top would stop it being one. So this page is
 * the documentation and the <iframe> below is the artifact, in its own
 * document, at its own width.
 *
 * That split is what lets both rules hold at once: every ROUTE on this site
 * carries the same menu, and every ARTIFACT stays the thing it demonstrates.
 *
 * GENERATED once from a table and authored from here on. Edit this file.
 */
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
---
<DocPage
  title="CODECAVE — Newsletter"
  nav="examples"
  sub="newsletter"
  stylesheets={['examples.css']}
>
<div class="ex">

  <header class="ex-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Email</p>
    <h1>Newsletter</h1>
    <p class="ex-lead">The longer-form sibling of the transactional email — a masthead, stacked story blocks and a footer, on the same table scaffolding.</p>
    <p class="ex-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="raw/newsletter.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open standalone</a>
      <code>examples/raw/newsletter.html</code>
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="ex-frame" data-ratio="tall">
    <iframe src="raw/newsletter.html" title="Newsletter" loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>

  <section class="ex-notes">
    <h2>What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>That the system reads at length in a medium with no cascade. Every colour is on-palette and inlined; the rhythm comes from table cell padding rather than margins, because margin collapsing is not reliable across clients.</p>
    <h2>Same caveat as the transactional email</h2>
    <p>Colour literals are forced and correct. The type sizes are hand numbers that happen to look right, and nothing currently checks them against the ramp.</p>
  </section>

</div>
</DocPage>
